FGXUNIX(5)           UNIX Programmer's Manual          FGXUNIX(5)



NAME
     fgxunix - the kernel image, and the segments inside it

DESCRIPTION
     /fgxunix is the whole kernel: the core bank, the far bank,
     the tty bank and the boot-essential modules, in one file.
     Stage 2 reads it, places each segment in a bank of its own,
     and jumps to the core.  This is 2.11BSD's arrangement -- a
     single /unix whose overlays live inside it, sized by
     ov_siz[NOVL] in the header -- rather than a set of files
     named by a manifest, which is what FGX shipped until
     2026-09-05 as /KERNEL, /KERNELFAR, /KERNELTTY and three
     entries under /lib/modules driven by /etc/preload.conf.

     THE FIRST BLOCK IS THE SEGMENT TABLE.  It begins with the
     eight bytes `FGXUNIX1', a segment count, one reserved byte,
     and then five bytes per segment: a base selector, a bank
     offset from that base, the destination offset's high byte,
     and a 16-bit count of 512-byte blocks.  The payloads follow
     in table order, each padded to a block boundary.

     SIZES ONLY, AND NO FILE OFFSETS.  A segment's position in
     the file is the sum of the block counts before it, which is
     how 2.11BSD's boot program places an overlay.  An offset
     stored beside a size is one more number that can disagree
     with it.

     THE TABLE NAMES A BASE, NOT A BANK, because no bank is known
     until the Memory Manager has answered.  Selector 0 is the
     kernel handle and selector 1 the module window in the
     rest-of-OS claim (REST_KMOD_FIRST_OFF in include/kernel.s).

     FOUR MODULE SEGMENTS IS THE LIMIT, and it is a real one:
     that window is four banks and stage 2 does not bound its
     cursor, so a fifth would land on the kernel heap before a
     console existed to say so.  tools/mkfgxunix.py refuses such
     a table, and refuses a segment that runs past the end of its
     bank, so both are build errors rather than black screens.

     A SEGMENT IN HERE IS NOT AN ALTERNATIVE TO modload.conf(5).
     A module belongs in the image only if it must be live before
     the kernel can dispatch a file operation.  That is a cycle
     and not a preference: a module named in modload.conf(5) is
     read THROUGH a filesystem, so the filesystem can never be
     one of them.  Putting it in the image dissolves the cycle --
     an image segment is read through nothing.

     Order matters, as it does in modload.conf(5): ufs resolves
     vfs's vtable in its own mod_init and ufsdir resolves both.
     Stage 2 records the module segments' banks in table order
     and the kernel registers them down that list.

     A file whose first eight bytes are not the magic is refused
     with a message on the boot console, before any bank is
     written.  A missing /fgxunix is fatal: there is nothing left
     to report it with.

FILES
     /fgxunix
     /etc/boot.conf         names it; the default is /fgxunix

SEE ALSO
     boot.conf(5), modload.conf(5)



